### Justification
The claim that “99% of people recover” is technically supported by the estimated global infection fatality rate of 0.15–0.20% as of October 2020 ([PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33026101/)), implying a survival rate above 99%. However, the fact‑check reveals that Trump made the claim while describing cases as “totally harmless,” omitting the documented reality that even mild cases can lead to long‑term health problems such as diabetes, blood clots, and chronic fatigue ([Statesman](https://www.statesman.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/07/07/fact-check-are-99-of-coronavirus-cases-lsquototally-harmlessrsquo-as-trump-said/42604755/)). Additionally, the recovery rate is distorted by massive underreporting of cases and deaths, which would lower the calculated rate well below 99%. Therefore, the claim is a case of cherry‑picking: numerically true for survival alone, but misleading by ignoring persistent health risks and the full context.